  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1250 AM CDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nMonday through Saturday:\n\nSynopsis: Humid & active weather conditions will continue into at \nleast mid-week as the short term period's warm front becomes cold \nfrontal & slowly sags south through the state. Slow to at times \nstationary boundary speed will keep a warm & humid air mass in place \nacross the region, which will combine with frontal lift & any \npassing impulses aloft to support additional periods of shower and \nthunderstorm development through at least Tuesday night. Some storms \nwill need to be watched for locally heavy rainfall, as well as \nsevere weather hazards. Frontal boundary evolution from Wednesday \nforward becomes less certain, with some solutions pushing the \nboundary south of the areas & others keeping it stalled and/or \nreturning it back north as a warm front. Will be monitoring trends \nregarding boundary evolution, as its progression will be the \ndifference between a continued humid & active pattern versus a \ncooler & drier one through the second half of the upcoming work \nweek.  \n\nMonday through Tuesday: Expecting additional bouts of showers and \nstorms as the slow-moving surface front works through. Relatively \nfirm consensus across guidance points toward the boundary remaining \ndraped across the region through the entirety of this time frame, \nwhich makes afternoon/evening shower & thunderstorm chances a good \nbet each day. Favorable overlap of abundant instability & sufficient \ndeep layer shear suggests that more organized storms could \nmaterialize either and/or both days. Will thus need to be watching \nclosely for severe weather hazards (particularly gusty winds & hail) \nduring this time frame. Continued elevated precipitable water values \nin the 1.5-1.75\"+ range would be supportive for heavy downpours in \nany storm activity as well. While individual bouts of storms should \nbe progressive/of low hydrologic impact, will be monitoring for any \nsignals hinting at training/repeat storm development over the same \nareas. \n\nQuigley",
